    docs(backlog): PDF-fetch strategies, author-popover avatars, shared papers
    
    Five entries, three of them from surveying neighbouring projects.
    
    From bibtui (tgoelles/bib_tui, a Textual TUI over a .bib file — notes
    in chat-stuff/possible-collaboration-bibtui.md, which is gitignored):
    
    - Construct publisher PDF URLs arithmetically from the DOI, as their
      Copernicus path does. Measured: our chain already finds *a* PDF for
      10.5194/tc-17-1585-2023, but it returns a repository copy from HAL;
      construction gets the publisher's version of record with no API call
      at all. The win is quality of copy and independence from
      OpenAlex/Unpaywall, not coverage — worth stating, because measured
      against "did we find a PDF" it scores nothing. Mirror image of the
      existing "derive DOIs from publisher filenames" entry.
    - arXiv as a direct fetch strategy. Also not new coverage: OpenAlex
      already resolves 10.48550/arXiv.2006.11239 to the right URL. Kept
      for the narrower reasons — it works with the OpenAlex circuit
      breaker tripped, and reaches entries with an arxiv.org URL but no
      DOI, which the DOI-keyed chain cannot.
    
    From JabRef (still very actively developed — 6.0 alphas through 2026,
    three GSoC projects; notes in chat-stuff/competitors-jabref.md):
    
    - Citation insertion into the document being written, promoted from
      "consider" now that both JabRef and Paperlib have it. Scoped to the
      clipboard route deliberately: LibreOffice/Word plugins are a large
      brittle surface and JabRef has twenty years of it.
    
    UI:
    
    - Author avatars in the main-window author popover. The rationale is
      that an avatar is a *curation signal* — you only have one because
      you went and found it — so its job is scannability in a long author
      list. That drives the design: blank space rather than initials discs
      when absent, because sparseness is the whole point, and no
      reordering or trail-based marking, which would dilute it.
    - Shared papers between an author and a collaborator, on right-click
      (left-click already navigates). Records that the OpenAlex AND-syntax
      `authorships.author.id:A+B` silently returns only A's works — 79 for
      Emsley+Cowtan, exactly Emsley's solo total — where the repeated-key
      form returns 11, all verified to carry both authors. A + query looks
      plausible and is badly wrong, so a test should pin it.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

